# Safety-net sweep timer for the factory driver plane (issue #283).
#
# The spool watch (lemcentre-factory.path, PathModified) is edge-triggered
# and only fires on a WRITE to the spool directory. Two states therefore
# need a re-arm that no new write will provide:
#   - a failed/crashed driver start that left unconsumed *.job envelopes,
#   - a stale <id>.running claim whose wrapper is still waiting.
#
# This timer activates the SAME unit the path unit triggers
# (lemcentre-factory.service), on a wall-clock cadence:
#   - OnBootSec: after a reboot, envelopes that survived the shutdown (the
#     path event for them fired pre-boot and inotify is not persistent)
#     are picked up within 5 minutes, unattended.
#   - OnUnitInactiveSec: after every driver run (success OR failure), the
#     next safety sweep is 15 minutes out — bounded recovery for pending
#     envelopes and stale claims WITHOUT requiring a new spool write.
#
# With the fixed driver-process, an empty queue is a clean no-op (exit 0);
# the timer's cost in steady state is one instant no-op start per idle
# 15-minute window. Because the timer targets the same (oneshot) unit as
# the path watch, systemd's single-flight start coalescing preserves the
# serial-execution property: at most one driver-process runs at a time.
#
# Timing invariant (ops/lemcentre-runner/recovery-constants.json):
#   STALE_SECONDS(4500) + OnUnitInactiveSec(900) + AccuracySec(60)
#   <= DEADLINE_SECONDS(5580) - POLL_SECONDS(10)
# so a stale claim is swept to its fail-closed verdict while the waiting
# wrapper is still polling (see verify-recovery.sh, which re-checks this
# from the installed artifacts).
[Unit]
Description=Factory driver-plane safety sweep (stale claims + stranded envelopes)

[Timer]
OnBootSec=5min
OnUnitInactiveSec=15min
AccuracySec=1min
Unit=lemcentre-factory.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
